{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's claim that rational preference 'inevitably' smuggles in induction relies on a false dichotomy: it assumes the only alternative to probabilistic expectation is 'blind' choice. It fails to address Popper's actual solution—the metaphysical decision or rule of method to prefer the better-tested theory *without* assigning it higher probability of truth. By ignoring this specific non-inductive basis for action (the willingness to act on the best available criticism-resistant theory while admitting it may fail), the target attacks a straw man rather than Popper's actual position on the defensible nature of scientific preference.","premise":"Popper distinguishes between the logical status of a theory (which cannot be made probable) and the pragmatic rule of action (prefer the best tested), grounding the latter in a critical attitude rather than an inductive expectation of success.","premises_essential":["d811afa95acdf601f66b12132d20acc9e0b09386022589039b752b0e26717e9c"],"successor_question":"If the preference is based on a metaphysical decision rather than an expectation of success, does this successfully avoid the charge of smuggling in induction, or does the decision itself rely on an implicit inductive hope?","target":"d811afa95acdf601f66b12132d20acc9e0b09386022589039b752b0e26717e9c"}]}